{"id": 97069, "date": "2017-09-11T20:31:27", "date_gmt": "2017-09-12T00:31:27", "guid": {"rendered": "https://www.nasa.gov/?migration=converted-node-409365"}, "modified": "2023-07-26T12:14:58", "modified_gmt": "2023-07-26T16:14:58", "slug": "converted-node-409365", "status": "publish", "type": "migration", "link": "https://www.nasa.gov/?migration=converted-node-409365", "title": {"rendered": "Microgravity Science Glovebox"}, "content": {"rendered": "<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-none \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg\"><img fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1363\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?w=2048\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"Expedition 47 Commander Tim Kopra configures the station\u2019s Microgravity Science Glovebox for upcoming research operations, 2016.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"eager\" parallax_attr=\"\" parallax_off=\"1\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg 4928w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=768,511 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=1024,682 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=1536,1022 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=2048,1363 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=400,266 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=600,399 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=900,599 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=1200,799 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss047e050514orig.jpg?resize=2000,1331 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">Expedition 47 Commander Tim Kopra configures the station\u2019s Microgravity Science Glovebox for upcoming research operations, 2016. <strong>Credits: NASA</strong></div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-none \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1363\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?w=2048\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" parallax_attr=\"\" parallax_off=\"1\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg 4928w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=768,511 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=1024,682 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=1536,1022 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=2048,1363 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=400,266 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=600,399 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=900,599 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=1200,799 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/iss042e046523.jpg?resize=2000,1331 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">JSC2014-E-042123 (8 May 2014) \u2014 At the Gagarin Cosmonaut Training Center in Star City, Russia, Expedition 40/41 backup crew member Samantha Cristoforetti of the European Space Agency (right) signs a ceremonial book at the Gagarin Museum May 8 as her Soyuz Commander, Anton Shkaplerov of the Russian Federal Space Agency (Roscosmos) looks on. Cristoforetti, Shkaplerov and Terry Virts of NASA are the backups to prime crew members Maxim Suraev of Roscosmos, Reid Wiseman of NASA and Alexander Gerst of the European Space Agency, who are preparing for launch May 29, Kazakh time, in the Soyuz TMA-13M spacecraft from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan for a 5 \u00bd month mission on the International Space Station. Photo credit: NASA</div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) is a sealed facility that provides an enclosed space for investigations conducted in a low-gravity, or microgravity, environment created on the International Space Station (ISS). The Glovebox accommodates small and medium-sized investigations from disciplines including biotechnology, combustion science, fluid physics, fundamental physics, and materials science. The MSG can provide the experiment with power, data acquisition, computer communications, vacuum, nitrogen, and specialized tools. It is designed to more closely simulate working laboratory conditions on the ground.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Glovebox provides a safe environment for Space Station crew to conduct research with liquids, flames, and particles used as a part of everyday research on Earth. Built-in gloves attached directly to the facility doors allow crew to safely manipulate samples inside the sealed facility, and side ports on the Glovebox permit crew to set up and manipulate investigation equipment inside. As experiments are conducted within the Glovebox by crew in space, the results can be monitored by scientists and investigators on the ground.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The development of the MSG on the ISS builds on successes with the Middeck Glovebox and Spacelab Glovebox, both used aboard several Space Shuttle missions and on the Russian Mir Space Station. The MSG supports larger, more sophisticated investigations than its predecessors, expanding research capabilities. It was developed by the European Space Agency (ESA) and is managed by NASA\u2019s Marshall Space Flight Center. The Glovebox was launched to the Space Station in June 2002 on Space Shuttle Endeavor STS-111 and is located in the ESA\u2019s Columbus laboratory module. Peggy Whitson installed the MSG during Expedition 5. Its first full operation experiment was the Solidification Using a Baffle in a Sealed Ampoule (SUBSA).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Resources</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-embed\"><div class=\"wp-block-embed__wrapper\">\nhttps://msglovebox.msfc.nasa.gov/home\n</div></figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-embed\"><div class=\"wp-block-embed__wrapper\">\nhttps://www.nasa.gov/centers/marshall/news/background/facts/MSG.html\n</div></figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-embed\"><div class=\"wp-block-embed__wrapper\">\nhttps://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/research/experiments/350.html\n</div></figure>\n", "protected": false}, "excerpt": {"rendered": "<p>The Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG) is a sealed facility that provides an enclosed space for investigations conducted in a low-gravity, or microgravity, environment created on the International Space Station (ISS). The Glovebox accommodates small and medium-sized investigations from disciplines including biotechnology, combustion science, fluid physics, fundamental physics, and materials science. The MSG can provide the [&hellip;]</p>\n", "protected": false}, "author": 35, "featured_media": 112227, "template": "", "meta": {"nasa_hds_core_meta_l10n": "en-US", "nasa_hds_core_meta_article_editor": "", "nasa_hds_core_meta_article_contacts": [], "nasa_hds_core_meta_article_topic": "", "nasa_hds_core_meta_linked_locations": [], "nasa_hds_core_meta_article_related_articles": [], "hds_migrate_meta_pr_release_id": "", "hds_migrate_meta_pr_release_type": "", "nasa_hds_core_meta_location_x": "", "nasa_hds_core_meta_location_y": "", "nasa_hds_core_meta_gallery_images": []}, "categories": [], "import-group": [8653, 8655, 8821], "import-terms": [], "internal-terms": [], "coauthors": [], "class_list": ["post-97069", "migration", "type-migration", "status-publish", "has-post-thumbnail", "hentry", "import-group-mars", "import-group-marshall-space-flight-center", "import-group-static-landing-pages"], "read_time": 3, "_links": {"self": [{"href": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/migration/97069", "targetHints": {"allow": ["GET"]}}], "collection": [{"href": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/migration"}], "about": [{"href": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/types/migration"}], "author": [{"embeddable": true, "href": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/users/35"}], "wp:featuredmedia": [{"embeddable": true, "href": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/media/112227"}], "wp:attachment": [{"href": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/media?parent=97069"}], "wp:term": [{"taxonomy": "category", "embeddable": true, "href": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/categories?post=97069"}, {"taxonomy": "import-group", "embeddable